Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (c) The Company Board shall, prior to making any recommendation change, determine in good faith, after thorough consultation with its financial advisor and legal counsel, that the proposed Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. The Board must consider the financial terms and any potential legal implications, ensuring that the proposal aligns with the fiduciary duties owed under applicable law. If the legal counsel advises that the proposal might lead to potential legal challenges, the Board must weigh these risks against the financial benefits of the proposal. Only if the financial benefits significantly outweigh the legal risks, after thorough consultation, can the Board determine the proposal to be superior. Additionally, the Board must provide Parent with a written notice of its intent to change recommendation, allowing for a negotiation period of five Business Days. If during this period, Parent makes a counterproposal that addresses the legal concerns while maintaining financial competitiveness, the Board must reconsider its stance. (Pages 88-89)
