Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (c) The Company Board shall evaluate any Acquisition Proposal in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, financial advisors, and strategic consultants, to determine if such proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. The evaluation shall consider, among other factors, (i) the financial terms of the proposal, (ii) the strategic benefits of the transaction, (iii) the potential impact on stakeholders, and (iv) the likelihood of obtaining necessary regulatory approvals. (d) If the Company Board determines that an Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, it must notify the Parent and engage in good faith negotiations to amend the existing agreement to match or exceed the terms of the Superior Proposal. (e) The Company Board's determination must be consistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable law, taking into account the long-term interests of the Company and its stockholders. (Pages 45-46)\nAnswer:
Choices:

