Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.4. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (c) In evaluating whether an Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal, the Company Board shall consider, in good faith, the financial terms of the proposal, the strategic objectives of the Company, and the long-term impact on the Company’s stakeholders. (d) The Company Board must also consult with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation to ensure that the proposal aligns with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable law. (e) The Company Board shall provide written notice to Parent of its intention to consider an Acquisition Proposal as superior, including a summary of the material terms and conditions of the proposal. (f) The Company Board shall negotiate in good faith with Parent to offer them the opportunity to propose adjustments to the terms of this Agreement, such that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer be considered superior. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
