Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (e) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may change its recommendation regarding an Acquisition Proposal at any time during the Pre-Closing Period if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and a strategic advisor, that the failure to make such a change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with a written notice of its intention to change its recommendation at least three Business Days prior to taking such action, and (iii) the Company Board has determined that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal after considering not only the financial aspects but also the strategic fit, regulatory implications, and potential impact on stakeholders, including employees and customers. Furthermore, the Company shall have engaged in good faith negotiations with Parent to amend the terms of the current Agreement to address the concerns raised by the Acquisition Proposal. (Pages 102-103)\nAnswer:
