Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (f) The Company Board, in determining whether an Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal, shall consider (i) the financial terms of the proposal, including the impact on shareholder value, (ii) the strategic alignment and potential synergies with the Company's long-term goals, (iii) the advice of the Company's outside legal counsel on regulatory and legal implications, and (iv) the potential impact on the Company's employees and community relations. The Company Board must act in good faith and in a manner consistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law. The Company shall provide the Parent with written notice of its intention to consider an Acquisition Proposal not less than five Business Days prior to making any recommendations. During this period, the Company shall engage in good faith negotiations with Parent to amend the existing agreement to make it more favorable. After considering these factors, the Company Board may determine in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
Choices:
